// BT-3744 (ADR 0131 Phase 0a): the REPL column of the ADR 0131 probe matrix.
//
// The REPL threads outer locals through its bindings map (the `MethodBody
// (REPL)` row of ADR 0131 §2). Every probe shape here writes the REPL local
// `t` inside a threading construct. The ADR's §"REPL and error examples"
// transcript is the approved display; it comes first, verbatim, followed by
// the o- and s-shapes of the ADR's two probe tables and the §1a sequencing
// shape. Shapes that are not REPL-expressible (o5/o12/o13 write a field, o6/o10
// return with `^` from a method) are covered by the BUnit matrix in
// stdlib/test/adr0131local_rebind_test.bt.
//
// An expression whose answer differs from the ADR today pins today's answer
// under a `PIN-BUG BT-3743` marker that names the correct answer. Each phase
// of the BT-3743 epic flips the pins it fixes; phase 5 (BT-3751) flips the
// rest. Each shape resets `t` first, so one shape's wrong answer does not
// change the next shape's input (the ADR transcript is the exception: it is
// one stateful sequence, as the ADR writes it).
//
// Since BT-3745 (ADR 0131 Phase 0b) every shape that crashed or answered
// wrong is a compile error (the Phase 0 allow-set error, or §6), so its pin
// asserts that error; a rejected input runs nothing, so the `t` that follows
// it still reads the value before it.

// ===========================================================================
// ADR 0131 §"REPL and error examples" (approved display)
// ===========================================================================

t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: the `on:do:` operand leaked its `{Value, StateAcc}` tuple
// (o1); since BT-3745 it is an ADR 0131 Phase 0 compile error. Correct
// answer: 2.
r := ([t := t + 1. 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0]) + 1
// => ERROR: `on:do:` writes outer local `t`, but as the receiver of a message

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 1 (the on:do: body's write is kept).
t
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: `collect:` in receiver position raised (o4); since
// BT-3745 it is a compile error. Correct answer: 2.
(#(1, 2) collect: [:x | t := t + x]) size
// => ERROR: `collect:` writes outer local `t`, but as the receiver of a message

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 4.
t
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: the Tier 2 block reaches `tryDo:`'s arity-0 native (ADR
// 0131 §5). Correct answer: true.
(Result tryDo: [t := t + 1. 1 / 0]) isError
// => ERROR: tryDo: expected a zero-arity block, got: Block/1

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 4 (the protected region raised, so its
// write is discarded). Today 0, because every write above was lost.
t
// => 0

// ===========================================================================
// o-shapes (operand position)
// ===========================================================================

// o2: `ensure:` as a binary operand.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: the cleanup block could not see `t`; since BT-3745 it is
// a compile error. Correct answer: 2.
r := ([t := t + 1. 1] ensure: [t := t + 1]) + 1
// => ERROR: `ensure:` writes outer local `t`, but as the receiver of a message

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 2.
t
// => 0

// o3: a read+write conditional as a binary operand.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: codegen referenced an unbound `State` (ADR 0131 §3);
// since BT-3745 it is a compile error. Correct answer: 2.
r := (4 =:= 5 ifTrue: [2] ifFalse: [t := t + 1. 1]) + 1
// => ERROR: `ifTrue:ifFalse:` writes outer local `t`, but as the receiver of a message

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 1.
t
// => 0

// o7: an `on:do:` nested in a conditional arm inside another `on:do:`.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: the inner tuple leaked, raised, and the outer handler
// answered 3 (a silent wrong answer); since BT-3745 it is a compile error.
// Correct answer: 7.
r := [false ifTrue: [2] ifFalse: [([t := t + 1. 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0]) + 1]. 7] on: Error do: [:x | 3]
// => ERROR: `on:do:` writes outer local `t`

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 1 (before BT-3745 the outer `on:do:`
// dropped `t` from the bindings).
t
// => 0

// o8: as o7 without the trailing `7`.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: a silent wrong answer (3) before BT-3745, a compile
// error since. Correct answer: 2.
r := [false ifTrue: [2] ifFalse: [([t := t + 1. 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0]) + 1]] on: Error do: [:x | 3]
// => ERROR: `on:do:` writes outer local `t`

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 1.
t
// => 0

// o11: the statement-position control. A conditional, then `ensure:`.
t := 0
// => 0

4 =:= 5 ifFalse: [t := t + 1]
// => 1

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: unlike the compiled contexts (where o11 passes), an
// `ensure:` whose body writes a REPL binding cannot see it even as a
// statement. Correct answer: 2.
[t := t + 1] ensure: [nil]
// => ERROR: Undefined variable: t

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 2.
t
// => 1

// ===========================================================================
// ADR 0131 §1a: a local read before a sibling rebinds it
// ===========================================================================

t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: the right operand leaked its tuple; since BT-3745 it is
// a compile error. Correct answer: 1 (source order reads `t` as 0 before the
// right operand writes it).
r := t + ([t := t + 1. 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0])
// => ERROR: `on:do:` writes outer local `t`, but as an argument of a message

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 1.
t
// => 0

// ===========================================================================
// s-shapes (assign-RHS and statement position)
// ===========================================================================

// s1: `at:ifAbsent:` as an assignment RHS.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: the Tier 2 block reached the native `at:ifAbsent:`;
// since BT-3745 it is an ADR 0131 §6 compile error until `at:ifAbsent:`
// becomes a producer (phase 2). Correct answer: 0.
r := #{#a => 1} at: #k ifAbsent: [t := t + 1. 0]
// => ERROR: block writes outer local `t`, but `at:ifAbsent:` cannot return the write

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 1.
t
// => 0

// s2: `detect:ifNone:` as an assignment RHS.
t := 0
// => 0

r := #(1, 2) detect: [:x | x > 5] ifNone: [t := t + 1. 0]
// => 0

t
// => 1

// s3: `inject:into:` as an assignment RHS.
t := 0
// => 0

r := #(1, 2) inject: 0 into: [:a :x | t := t + 1. a + x]
// => 3

t
// => 2

// s4: a stored closure, invoked directly.
t := 0
// => 0

b := [t := t + 1]
// => _

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: the stored Tier 2 block is sent `value` via perform:.
// ADR 0131 §6 makes this a compile error when the binding and the send are
// compiled together (BT-3745); across two REPL inputs the second sees `b`
// only as a binding, so the runtime error remains. Correct answer: 1.
b value
// => ERROR: Cannot call 'value' on Block via perform:

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 1 (or a compile error under ADR 0131 §6).
t
// => 0

// s5: `ifTrue:ifFalse:` as an assignment RHS.
t := 0
// => 0

r := 4 =:= 5 ifTrue: [2] ifFalse: [t := t + 1. 1]
// => 1

t
// => 1

// s6: an Erlang FFI argument: lossy by design (ADR 0041 §Erlang Interop
// Boundary), so the write is dropped and `t` stays 0.
t := 0
// => 0

r := (Erlang lists) map: [:x | t := t + 1. x] with: #(1)
// => [1]

t
// => 0

// s7: `ifNil:` as an assignment RHS.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: the Tier 2 block was called with the wrong arity; since
// BT-3745 it is a compile error. Correct answer: 1.
r := nil ifNil: [t := t + 1. 1]
// => ERROR: `ifNil:` writes outer local `t`, but as the value of an assignment

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 1.
t
// => 0

// s8: a statement-position `do:`.
t := 0
// => 0

#(1, 2) do: [:x | t := t + x]
// => _

t
// => 3

// s9: `reject:` in receiver position.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: raised `invalid argument` before BT-3745, a compile error
// since. Correct answer: 2.
r := (#(1, 2) reject: [:x | t := t + 1. false]) size
// => ERROR: `reject:` writes outer local `t`, but as the receiver of a message

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 2.
t
// => 0

// ===========================================================================
// BT-3753: statement-position constructs nested in a block
// ===========================================================================

// A `do:` in a conditional arm.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: leaked the bindings map as its value and left `t` at 0;
// since BT-3753 it is an ADR 0131 Phase 0 compile error. Correct answer: 3.
4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [#(1, 2) do: [:x | t := t + x]]
// => ERROR: `do:` writes outer local `t`, but as a statement inside a conditional arm

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 3.
t
// => 0

// A loop in a `do:` body.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: raised `invalid argument` before BT-3753, a compile error
// since. Correct answer: 2.
#(1) do: [:z | [t < 2] whileTrue: [t := t + 1]]
// => ERROR: `whileTrue:` writes outer local `t`, but as a statement inside a `do:` iteration

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 2.
t
// => 0

// `nil ifNil:` as a statement.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: called the block with the wrong arity before BT-3753, a
// compile error since. Correct answer: 1.
nil ifNil: [t := t + 1]
// => ERROR: `ifNil:` writes outer local `t`, but as a statement in a top-level expression

// PIN-BUG BT-3743: correct answer 1.
t
// => 0

// A conditional in a conditional arm answers right and is accepted.
t := 0
// => 0

4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [t := t + 1]]
// => _

t
// => 1
